# Discussion

In this study we evaluated the performance of confirmatory testing methods for \emph{edge-set} and \emph{edge-weight} null hypotheses. 
Generally, $\chi^2$-Tests and SEM fit statistics were able to detect edge-set misspecifications, unless the theoretical edge-set was a simple overspecification of the population edge-set. 
Parametric bootstrapping mostly increased performance, especially regarding type-I-errors. 
Tests and the SRMR fit statistic (when bootstrapped) were capable of detecting misspecifications of \emph{edge-weights}. 
For all cases, combining confirmatory testing with exploratory estimation methods (e.g. regularization) affected the results negatively.

## General performance of fit indices and tests

Our findings regarding SEM-based methods for testing edge-set hypotheses largely align with @du2025:
RMSEA performed best overall in terms of $\alpha$ error control, and the $\chi^2$ test overrejected well-fitting models more frequently at higher $p$.
In line with their findings on Type I fit indices, NFI performed worse than Type II and III indices.
However, as evidenced by irregular, $N$- and $p$-specific $\alpha$ error and power patterns, fixed cutoffs proved unsuitable in our study.

