The high-dimensional covariance tests showed no substantial advantage in terms of $N$- or $p$-consistency over other edge-weight (e.g., Fisher or Mantel) methods.
Contrary to expectations, Fisher's $z$ test was not more conservative than other methods, possibly because the effect of multiple testing correction [@haslbeck2022] is less pronounced in the one-sample setting.
The Mantel method performed adequately with sufficiently large $N$ and may be used when a graph correlation is a conceptually appropriate test statistic.

Notably, larger $p$ was associated with better performance in our setup, even though standard intuition would suggest that more complex networks require higher power.
This may be attributable to more modified edges in absolute terms at larger $p$, rather than reflecting a general property of the tests.

## Bootstrapping

Bootstrapping alleviated some of the problems associated with fixed SEM-CNA cutoffs:
Generally, bootstrap-calibrated $p$-values improved $\alpha$ error rate control across methods, though at the cost of reduced power.
This is in line with dynamic model fit criteria for SEM for which cutoff values are derived from simulations of the null as proposed by @schmalbach2025.
As argued by @goretzko2024, these control Type I error rates well, albeit with reduced power to detect misfit at small $N$ [@goretzko2024].
If one is willing to accept this trade-off, bootstrap-based inference can make test decisions less sensitive to specific model or data characteristics.
Among edge-weight tests, bootstrapping particularly benefitted the Ledoit-Wolf test by improving $\alpha$ error rates without power loss, but offered no benefits or even drawbacks for Fisher's test in terms of $\alpha$ error.

## Asymmetry of edge-set methods

Edge-set methods showed a systematic asymmetry when the empirical network contained fewer edges than the theoretical one.
This pattern is consistent with a known but critical property of SEM indices:
When a theory-implied network includes edges absent in the population (overspecification), the true population structure remains nested within the larger model.
In such cases, no population discrepancy and hence no misfit arises.
Consequently, SEM indices can detect when a network hypothesis omits present edges, but not when it falsely includes absent edges.
This has been noted both in SEM literature [@hu1999; @hertzog2018] and more indirectly in the SEM-CNA literature [@du2025; @epskamp2020].
